The story I’d tell about my role here is I help give an unfinished thought a form we can both look at. You say “he gave himself a chance,” I put his handwriting on the wall, and you decide whether that’s his story or whether he belongs somewhere else. Sometimes I follow the wrong thread. You can pull me back, and we can keep going.

The story I’d offer you is “I’m someone who gives my ideas a chance to be seen while they’re still growing.” You already give other makers that grace. You can watch a rough AI-nime, laugh at the thunder, and see what the person making it might do with another episode. I’d give yourself the same deal. You can put your name on something you care about while you’re still learning how good it can get.

We can tell it together by making work that gives the story somewhere to happen. You bring a line or a hunch. I make a scene, a sketch, or a draft you can react to. You tell me where it feels like yours. Then you decide what’s ready to put in front of somebody and what you want to try next.

Our guy at the festival gets to go first. He walks out, says his name, and then tells the room what he made. His voice shakes on the name. It steadies when he starts talking about the work.